## AQARION  

# Repository Security Audit  

Code vulnerabilities \(\cdot\) secrets exposure \(\cdot\) dependencies \(\cdot\) CI/CD \(\cdot\) evidence architecture  


<table>Repositoryquantarion369-arch/AQARIONReviewed snapshotmain at commit b44e6c6a18ddcaa8f23d69a7c886ce6408bc526bReview date2026-10-09 (system date)MethodGitHub API tree and source-file inspection; targeted code-search queries; static reasoningLocal cloneAttempted but blocked because this execution environment could not resolve github.com; review continued through GitHub connector access</table>  

## Executive assessment  

The reviewed repository shows several positive security practices: CI jobs explicitly set read- only repository contents permissions; workflows use bounded runtimes; the Mobile Research Kit validates manifest paths, rejects symlinks along listed paths, recomputes SHA- 256, escapes HTML output, and labels its output as process evidence rather than mathematical certification. Targeted secret- pattern searches returned no matches. No confirmed credential or direct remote- code- execution vulnerability was established in this review.  

The most actionable issues are a CI result- integrity weakness in the D22 workflow, an execution- trust boundary in the external replay controller when a supplied archive/policy is not independently authenticated, and a resource- exhaustion risk in the quadratic evidence verifier when fed untrusted reports with extreme coefficient bounds. Mutable GitHub Action tags, unpinned dependencies, and command/device details in local receipts are additional hardening gaps.  

Important limitation: this is a static remote- source audit, not a complete clone- based scan. No repository tests, dependency vulnerability database scan, Gitleaks/TruffleHog history scan, or dynamic exploit test was run here. Findings are therefore scoped to inspected source and metadata; absence of a finding is not proof of absence.

